WHAT IS CONTENT AND WHAT IS CODE

The line is drawn by the kind of material, not by the folder it sits in.

Curriculum content (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, this file) is teaching material: the text, cases,
questions, answers and rationales, feedback, scripts and other educational material
written to teach. It lives mainly in:

1. The numbered content trees, 00_START_HERE/ through 14_Tracks/ and 99_Archive/.
2. The root registries that carry curriculum material, for example topic_meta.json,
   question_bank.json, curriculum.json, communication_cases.json, reasoning_cases.json,
   reasoning_cases_resident.json, longitudinal_case.json, family_systems_scenarios.json,
   pairings.json, evidence_registry.json and evidence_annotations.json.
3. The learner-facing text in _prototypes/, such as the scenario and standardized-patient
   case packs (*.pack.json) and the text of the tools that ship to learners.
4. The built learner pages of both sites (the output of
   13_Faculty_Resources/_automation/site_build/build_and_check.sh).

Curriculum content stays content wherever it is copied. That includes the
curriculum-review transcripts in docs/curriculum-review/, test snapshots and fixtures (for
example tests/__panels__/), benchmark corpora (benchmarks/), case-pack text quoted in
tests, and documentation that quotes a page.

Code (the MIT License, in LICENSE) is software and its configuration: source files (for
example .py, .js, .mjs, .jsx and .sh), every *.schema.json, build, test and CI
configuration, and the documentation of how the software works. Code is code wherever it
lives, including inside the numbered trees (for example the build scripts under
13_Faculty_Resources/_automation/ and the question-bank engine under
09_Exam_Prep/shelf_comat_bank/engine/). It never includes the curriculum content copied
into it.

When one file holds both, each part keeps its own licence. In the single-file HTML
clinical tools, for example, the program logic is code and the teaching text, cases,
questions and feedback are content.


